<LearnModule slug="uncertainty/bayesian-mcmc" description="Interactive Bayesian calibration of Newton's law of cooling with a Metropolis Markov chain Monte Carlo sampler: chain trace, posterior samples, credible intervals and posterior predictive band.">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Not one answer, but a distribution of answers</h2>
    <p>A hot object cools towards the ambient temperature. With Newton’s law of cooling its temperature is</p>
    <Equation tex={String.raw`T(t) = T_\infty + (T_0 - T_\infty)\, e^{-t/\tau},`} />
    <p>where <em>T</em><sub>0</sub> = 90 °C is known, while the time constant <em>τ</em> (which depends on the heat transfer coefficient) and the ambient temperature <em>T</em><sub>∞</sub> are not. A thermocouple gives a few noisy readings. A least-squares fit returns one pair (<em>τ</em>, <em>T</em><sub>∞</sub>). The Bayesian question is different: <em>which values are compatible with the data, and how compatible?</em></p>
    <p>Bayes’ theorem combines what the data say (the likelihood) with what we knew before (the prior). With Gaussian noise of known size <em>σ</em> and a uniform prior on a plausible box,</p>
    <Equation tex={String.raw`p(\tau, T_\infty \mid d) \;\propto\; p(d \mid \tau, T_\infty)\, p(\tau, T_\infty),\qquad p(d \mid \tau, T_\infty) \propto \exp\!\Big(-\frac{1}{2\sigma^2}\sum_i r_i^2\Big),\qquad r_i = d_i - T(t_i;\tau,T_\infty).`} />
    <p>The normalising constant is an integral we would rather not compute. Markov chain Monte Carlo avoids it: it produces samples whose histogram converges to the posterior, using only ratios of the formula above.</p>
    <h2>The Metropolis algorithm</h2>
    <p>From the current point, propose a random step of typical size <em>w</em>. Accept it with probability min(1, <em>p</em>(proposal)/<em>p</em>(current)); otherwise stay where you are and record the current point again. Uphill moves are always accepted, downhill moves sometimes, so the chain spends time in each region in proportion to its probability.</p>
  </div>

  <McmcExplorer />

  <div class="try">
    <h3>Exercises</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Run the sampler. The chain starts far away (τ = 30 min, T∞ = 50 °C). How many iterations does it need to reach the high-probability region? This is the <em>burn-in</em>, which is discarded.
        <Answer lang="en"><p>With the defaults the chain reaches the cloud around τ ≈ 9 min and T∞ ≈ 22 °C after roughly a hundred iterations, and the trace then settles into a noisy band. Samples taken before that point describe the starting guess, not the data, so they are dropped. The lab discards the first 20 % of the chain (at most 1000 samples), which is on the safe side.</p></Answer></li>
      <li>Set the proposal width very small (log₁₀ w = −2), then very large (+1.5). Watch the acceptance rate and the trace. Why are both extremes inefficient? Values of about 20–50 % are usually a good target.
        <Answer lang="en"><p>With very small steps almost every proposal is accepted (about 90 %), but each move is tiny: the chain crawls and may not even reach the high-probability region in 20 000 iterations. With very large steps almost every proposal lands in an improbable place and is rejected (acceptance near 0 %), so the chain stays at the same point for long stretches, visible as flat segments in the trace. In both cases consecutive samples are nearly identical and carry little new information. The default step gives about 40 %.</p></Answer></li>
      <li>Keep only readings up to 5 min. The posterior samples form a long, tilted cloud: <em>τ</em> and <em>T</em>∞ are correlated. Why can a slow cooling towards a low ambient look like a fast cooling towards a high one when the experiment is short?
        <Answer lang="en"><p>At the start of the experiment the curve is almost a straight line whose slope is (T₀ − T∞)/τ. A lower ambient temperature (larger T₀ − T∞) combined with a larger τ gives the same slope, so the early readings cannot tell the two apart. With readings up to 5 min the correlation between τ and T∞ is about −0.98 and T∞ is only known to lie somewhere between about 1 and 35 °C. Only readings near the final plateau measure T∞ directly.</p></Answer></li>
      <li>Extend the readings to 40 min and compare the width of the credible intervals. Which parameter benefited most from the longer experiment?
        <Answer lang="en"><p>T∞. Going from 15 to 40 min narrows its 95 % interval from about 11 °C wide to about 3 °C, while the interval for τ shrinks from about 2.7 to 1.6 min. The late readings sit on the plateau, which is set by T∞ alone, and they also break the correlation between the two parameters.</p></Answer></li>
      <li>Increase the noise. Does the 95 % band on the data plot still contain the true curve? It should most of the time, but not always: that is what 95 % means.
        <Answer lang="en"><p>The band widens as the noise grows, because more parameter values are compatible with the readings. For most data sets it still contains the true curve, but not at every time and not for every data set: a 95 % band is expected to miss the truth about 5 % of the time. Press “New data” a few times to see this.</p></Answer></li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What the samples give you</h2>
    <p>Every question about the parameters becomes an average over samples: the median and a 95 % credible interval come from sorting them; the <em>posterior predictive band</em> comes from evaluating the model for many samples and taking quantiles at each time. The correlation between parameters, invisible in a table of ± values, is visible in the cloud.</p>
    <p>Metropolis is the simplest MCMC method; it slows down in many dimensions and in narrow, curved posteriors, which is why methods such as Hamiltonian Monte Carlo use gradients. The same idea, a distribution instead of a single answer, is what uncertainty quantification brings to the reconstructions in the <a href={url('/learn/inverse-problems/abel-transform/')}>Abel module</a> and to <a href={url('/learn/machine-learning/physics-informed-learning/')}>physics-informed networks</a>.</p>
  </div>
</LearnModule>
